Concurrent hole-refill lifetime test
===================================

  sudo <userpages4k launcher> ./anon-hole-refill-race

Run with a 4K userspace ABI on the native 16K kernel. CAP_SYS_ADMIN is required
to distinguish real swap entries from migration and poison markers. The target
configuration has MAX_SWAPFILES=27; types 27 through 31 are excluded and counted
separately.

Four workers own disjoint 16K virtual windows in a 16 MiB region. Two phased
rounds discard and refill individual 4K leaves. Barriers synchronize quarter
phases, exercising concurrent shared-pool reservations without a userspace data
race. Every refill checks zeroing and unchanged neighbors. This covers pool
contention across windows, not simultaneous PTE changes within one window.

After joining workers and verifying the region, the allocating process forks a
survivor, modifies one native window privately and exits. A supervisor waits
for that exit before releasing the survivor. The survivor verifies original
contents, rewrites all leaf tags, and verifies them again. This checks that
backing survives its original allocating mm and preserves fork isolation.

The survivor then applies MADV_COLD/PAGEOUT only to its own region, at most 20
times with 10 ms between attempts. At least one real swapped leaf and complete
byte-exact readback are required for the reclaim result. The reported count
states coverage; one swapped leaf is not broad reclaim qualification. These
advice operations can skip nonlinear pooled folios because their mappings are
not contiguous. Use README.hole-refill-reclaim for broader controlled coverage.

Expect three TAP passes and no skips or unexpected signals. The supervisor has
a 35-second alarm and children have 30-second alarms. Failure or timeout kills
the dedicated process group; the supervisor reaps surviving descendants. An
external runner should also bound the entire fixture cgroup.
